#### 1. Decorators

Decorators are a way to modify or annotate classes and class members. They are still a proposal and not part of the official JavaScript standard yet, but they are widely used in frameworks like Angular and libraries like TypeScript.

**Example of a Class Decorator:**

```javascript
function sealed(constructor) {
  Object.seal(constructor);
  Object.seal(constructor.prototype);
}

@sealed
class Greeter {
  constructor(public greeting: string) {}
  greet() {
    return `Hello, ${this.greeting}`;
  }
}
```

**Example of a Method Decorator:**

```javascript
function log(target, propertyKey, descriptor) {
  const originalMethod = descriptor.value;
  descriptor.value = function (...args) {
    console.log(`Calling ${propertyKey} with arguments:`, args);
    return originalMethod.apply(this, args);
  };
  return descriptor;
}

class Calculator {
  @log
  add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
  }
}
```

#### 2. Email Addresses

In string literals, the `@` symbol is used to separate the local part from the domain part of an email address.

**Example:**

```javascript
const email = "user@example.com";
console.log(email); // Output: user@example.com
```

